  <a href="#section-observation-options" id="section-observation-options" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Choose how to observe sending</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Progress, final results and telemetry answer different questions. Use them independently or together:</p>
    <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Compare mail-send observation facilities"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Facility</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>What you get</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><a href="#section-live-progress">Progress observer</a></td><td>What is happening now?</td>
          <td>Stages and available upload counts, with finer SMTP detail when supported. Updates can be combined or dropped, so this is a best-effort view, not an audit trail.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><a href="#section-observer-results">Result observer</a></td><td>What happened, and where did the time go?</td>
          <td>The final outcome, receipt, failure, portable timings and bounded connection/submission histories.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><a href="/opentelemetry.html">OpenTelemetry</a></td><td>How is sending behaving across my application?</td>
          <td>Application-linked traces, per-send timings and aggregate measurements, recorded independently of observer delivery.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>The result observer and telemetry reuse the same portable measurement boundaries. Detailed progress can identify greeting,
      TLS, authentication and other SMTP stages, but it does not turn them into separate duration measurements.</p>
    <p>Intermediate upload-count updates are throttled to one every 250 milliseconds; stage changes and the final count bypass that throttle
      but can still be combined.
      Neither that reporting interval nor your telemetry export interval rounds the underlying send timings.
      All three facilities describe local sending and SMTP submission, not final mailbox delivery.</p>
  </div></section>

  <a href="#section-get-receipt" id="section-get-receipt" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Get the receipt, including when a send fails</h2>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        Every single-email send produces a receipt. <code>mailer.sync().sendMail(email)</code> returns it directly; ignore it when only completion matters. It follows the ordinary synchronous send
        path, but returns a <code>MailSubmissionReceipt</code>. A failed or partial submission throws <code>MailSubmissionException</code> instead;
        <code>getSubmissionReceipt()</code> gives you the facts from that same attempt. The exception's <code>getStatus()</code> delegates to the
        receipt, and <code>getCause()</code> retains the original Jakarta Mail failure.
      </p>
      <p>
        Catching only <code>MailSubmissionException</code> is deliberate here. Preparation and validation failures, for example, do not produce a
        submission receipt and still propagate to the caller. Connection acquisition and exhausted failover routes also fail without a receipt.
        A provider-internal preflight failure can have a receipt without a server response; receipt presence alone does not prove an SMTP command was reached.
      </p>
      <p>
        The snippets below reuse a configured <code>mailer</code> and an <code>email</code>, and omit imports. <code>printReceipt</code> is a small
        application method defined in the next example, not another Simple Java Mail API.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small" id="example-get-receipt">MailSubmissionReceipt receipt;
try {
    receipt = mailer.sync().sendMail(email);
} catch (MailSubmissionException failure) {
    receipt = failure.getSubmissionReceipt();
}

printReceipt(receipt);</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <h3>Two levels, four getters</h3>
      <p>
        Two getters describe the whole attempt. Each <code>recipient</code> in <code>receipt.getRecipientResults()</code> exposes two more:
      </p>
      <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable send result getter overview">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Getter</th><th>Returned enum</th><th>Question it answers</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>receipt.getStatus()</code></td><td><code>MailSubmissionStatus</code></td><td>Was the whole attempt accepted, partially accepted, rejected or unknown?</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>receipt.getRetryDisposition()</code></td><td><code>MailRetryDisposition</code></td><td>What do the recorded facts say about retrying this envelope?</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>recipient.getDisposition()</code></td><td><code>MailRecipientDisposition</code></td><td>What is the final submission result for this recipient?</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>recipient.getRcptStatus()</code></td><td><code>SmtpRecipientStatus</code></td><td>What happened at <code>RCPT TO</code> for this address?</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        <code>ACCEPTED</code> at <code>RCPT TO</code> only means the server accepted the address. An <code>ACCEPTED</code> recipient disposition
        means the message was accepted for that recipient. An <code>ACCEPTED</code> receipt means that happened for every submitted recipient and
        the send completed successfully. None of these confirms mailbox delivery.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable submission status reference">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th><code>receipt.getStatus()</code></th><th>What happened</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>ACCEPTED</code></td><td>Every submitted envelope recipient was accepted, and the send completed successfully.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>PARTIALLY_ACCEPTED</code></td><td>At least one recipient was accepted, but the attempt was not a clean success for the whole envelope. The send fails.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>REJECTED</code></td><td>No recipient was accepted for submission. The send fails; inspect the replies and retry guidance for the reason.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>UNKNOWN</code></td><td>The send path cannot determine acceptance. This can describe a failed SMTP attempt or successful processing in logging-only mode or a custom mailer.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        <code>getEmailId()</code> returns the effective Message-ID, or <code>null</code> if none was produced.
        <code>getSubmittedAt()</code> is the timestamp captured after the attempt completed or failed, not a delivery timestamp.
        <code>getSmtpResponse()</code> is an optional provider response; a positive reply alone does not replace the overall status and recipient facts.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>isRequireTlsUsed()</code> tells you whether the first server was actually sent the RFC 8689 REQUIRETLS parameter for this attempt.
        It can be true even when that server rejected <code>MAIL FROM</code>, and false when a required send failed locally before that command.
        It does not claim that later relays complied. See <a href="security.html#section-requiretls">Require TLS for onward delivery</a> for the
        configuration and trust checks behind this value.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>isAcceptedByServer()</code> means <em>at least one</em> recipient was accepted, so it is also true for <code>PARTIALLY_ACCEPTED</code>.
        <code>hasServerAcceptanceInformation()</code> is false for <code>UNKNOWN</code>. Neither method is a shortcut for confirming that everyone
        received the message.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-message-size" id="section-message-size" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Check message size against the server limit</h2>
  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>An attachment can make an email too large for your SMTP server, even if your own application allows it.
        On managed Angus connections, Simple Java Mail counts the prepared email and checks the server's advertised SIZE limit
        before submitting anything. There is no switch to enable: ordinary sends, async sends, simple batches and open connections all use this check.</p>
      <p>These two receipt fields tell you what happened, including when the check rejects an email:</p>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small">MailSubmissionReceipt receipt;
try {
    receipt = mailer.sync().sendMail(email);
} catch (MailSubmissionException failure) {
    receipt = failure.getSubmissionReceipt();
}

System.out.println("Submission: " + receipt.getStatus());
System.out.println("Prepared content bytes: " + receipt.getMessageSize());
System.out.println("Server limit bytes: " + receipt.getServerMaximumMessageSize());
System.out.println("SMTP reply present: " + receipt.getSmtpResponse().isPresent());</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-text small">Submission: REJECTED
Prepared content bytes: 12345678
Server limit bytes: 10000000
SMTP reply present: false</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>Here the email was too large, so no MAIL FROM, RCPT or DATA command was sent. The exception explains that you can reduce
        the content or attachments, or use a server with a higher limit. Its receipt lists the recipients as valid but unsent, with
        <code>CALLER_POLICY_REQUIRED</code> retry guidance. Retrying the same email against the same limit will not help.</p>
      <p><code>getMessageSize()</code> is the complete prepared SMTP content size, not bytes successfully transferred. It includes SMTP line
        endings and excludes dot escaping and the DATA terminator. Counting happens after negotiated provider conversion, without another
        buffered copy; exact and signed content is not rebuilt. Caller-owned attachment and content sources must stay readable and return
        the same content on repeated reads.</p>
      <p><code>getServerMaximumMessageSize()</code> describes the actual selected connection, using the capabilities after STARTTLS when applicable.
        Equality is allowed. Missing, zero, malformed, overflowing or contradictory limits are unknown and do not trigger rejection.
        The client adds SIZE when advertised; one valid advanced caller-supplied estimate is preserved, but the receipt still reports the measured size.</p>
      <p>Either field can be <code>null</code>. Incomplete measurement does not become a partial count. Logging-only sends, CustomMailer,
        ordinary caller-owned Angus Sessions and providers that do not report sizes leave these facts unavailable. A known server limit can
        survive a failure that happened before counting finished. Being under the limit does not guarantee acceptance or final delivery.</p>
      <p>This is separate from your application's <code>withMaximumEmailSize(...)</code> guard and
        <code>mailer.rehearse(email).getEncodedSize()</code>, which work without a server. Rehearsal EML can differ from SMTP content after
        provider conversion or omitted headers. A <a href="/debugging.html#section-smtp-capabilities">connection probe</a> is useful for diagnosis,
        but may connect to a different server in a cluster; sending always checks its own connection.</p>
      <p>To collect these facts for successful and failed attempts in one place, use your result observer. Attempts that fail before a receipt
        exists do not enter this callback's <code>ifPresent</code> block:</p>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small">mailerBuilder.withMailSendResultObserver(outcome -&gt;
    outcome.getSubmissionReceipt().ifPresent(receipt -&gt;
        System.out.printf("%s: content=%s bytes, server limit=%s bytes%n",
            receipt.getStatus(), receipt.getMessageSize(),
            receipt.getServerMaximumMessageSize())));</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-text small">ACCEPTED: content=3250 bytes, server limit=10000000 bytes
REJECTED: content=12345678 bytes, server limit=10000000 bytes</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-partial-send" id="section-partial-send" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Choose what happens when a recipient is rejected</h2>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        Suppose an email is addressed to Alice, Bob and Carol, in that order. The server accepts Alice's address, temporarily rejects Bob's, and
        permanently rejects Carol's. You can stop there without sending content to anyone, or continue for Alice alone.
        This is one choice for the whole Email, not a separate preference on each recipient.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use <code>withSendingToAcceptedRecipients(true)</code> to continue, or <code>false</code> to withhold content after a known recipient rejection.
        Continuing does not turn this into a successful send: Bob and Carol were still rejected, so the operation fails with its partial receipt.
        Stopping is not atomic delivery and cannot prevent a later bounce after an otherwise accepted submission.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small" id="example-partial-config">Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.org", 587, "username", "password")
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
    .buildMailer();

Email email = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .from("sender@example.org")
    .withRecipients(to("Alice", "alice@example.org"), to("Bob", "bob@example.org"),
        to("Carol", "carol@example.org"))
    .withSubject("An update for all three recipients")
    .withPlainText("The message content")
    .withSendingToAcceptedRecipients(true)
    .buildEmail();

try {
    printReceipt(mailer.sync().sendMail(email));
} catch (MailSubmissionException failure) {
    printReceipt(failure.getSubmissionReceipt());
}

// Use false instead to stop after a recipient rejection.
// Reuse the Mailer for your workload; close it when finished.</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-text small">RCPT TO:&lt;alice@example.org&gt;  → 250 2.1.5 Recipient OK
RCPT TO:&lt;bob@example.org&gt;    → 450 4.2.0 Try again later
RCPT TO:&lt;carol@example.org&gt;  → 550 5.1.1 No such mailbox
DATA                         → 354 Send message data
[message data, then a dot]    → 250 2.0.0 Queued as A17</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>With <code>false</code>, the same recipient replies produce no <code>DATA</code> command. Alice's positive address reply is retained,
        but her content remains unsent. The call still throws <code>MailSubmissionException</code>, this time with a <code>REJECTED</code> receipt:</p>
<pre><code class="language-text small">withSendingToAcceptedRecipients(false):
Submission: REJECTED
Retry: SAFE_TO_RETRY_UNACCEPTED
alice@example.org: rcpt=ACCEPTED (250), submitted=VALID_UNSENT
bob@example.org: rcpt=TEMPORARILY_REJECTED (450), submitted=VALID_UNSENT
carol@example.org: rcpt=PERMANENTLY_REJECTED (550), submitted=INVALID
[no message content transmitted]</code></pre>
      <p>A later retry may include Alice and Bob, not Carol's unchanged address. When all recipients are rejected, neither choice sends content.
        Neither choice retries automatically, splits the Email or removes addresses from its visible headers.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>
        Back to the <code>true</code> case: the final <code>250</code> confirms that Alice's submission was accepted. It does not undo Bob's or Carol's rejection. The call throws
        <code>MailSubmissionException</code> with a <code>PARTIALLY_ACCEPTED</code> receipt. The earlier catch block passes that receipt to this method:
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small" id="example-print-receipt">private static void printReceipt(MailSubmissionReceipt receipt) {
    System.out.println("Submission: " + receipt.getStatus());
    System.out.println("Retry: " + receipt.getRetryDisposition());

    for (MailRecipientResult recipient : receipt.getRecipientResults()) {
        String address = recipient.getEnvelopeAddress()
            .orElse(recipient.getOriginalAddress());
        String code = recipient.getRcptResponse()
            .map(reply -&gt; Integer.toString(reply.getReturnCode()))
            .orElse("unavailable");

        System.out.printf("%s: rcpt=%s (%s), submitted=%s%n",
            address, recipient.getRcptStatus(), code,
            recipient.getDisposition());
    }
}</code></pre>
      <strong>Illustrative output for this attempt</strong>
<pre><code class="language-text small" id="output-partial-send">Submission: PARTIALLY_ACCEPTED
Retry: SAFE_TO_RETRY_UNACCEPTED
alice@example.org: rcpt=ACCEPTED (250), submitted=ACCEPTED
bob@example.org: rcpt=TEMPORARILY_REJECTED (450), submitted=VALID_UNSENT
carol@example.org: rcpt=PERMANENTLY_REJECTED (550), submitted=INVALID</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="wide">
      <ul class="indent">
        <li><strong>Alice:</strong> do not resend her copy. The server already accepted it.</li>
        <li><strong>Bob:</strong> his copy was not submitted. A later attempt may succeed.</li>
        <li><strong>Carol:</strong> this reply requires attention to the address; repeating the same attempt is not the next step.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The older <code>getAcceptedRecipients()</code>, <code>getValidUnsentRecipients()</code> and <code>getInvalidRecipients()</code> lists remain
        available as immutable address lists derived from the same recipient results. Prefer the individual results when the reason matters:
        being in <code>getValidUnsentRecipients()</code> does not, on its own, make an address retryable.
      </p>
      <p>The same failed-completion contract applies to <code>mailer.async().sendMail(email)</code>. Your result observer receives
        <code>isSuccessful() == false</code> and the same receipt. A simple batch still stops at its first failed email, even when that email was
        partially accepted. A lost final server reply can still mean <code>UNKNOWN</code>; permission to continue does not remove duplicate risk.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <h3>Defaults, clearing and provider compatibility</h3>
      <p>The setting on the Email starts unset. Calling <code>withSendingToAcceptedRecipients(...)</code> replaces the value on its builder;
        <code>clearSendingToAcceptedRecipients()</code> removes that value rather than choosing false.
        The existing <a href="configuration.html#section-config-mailer">Email defaults and overrides</a> can then supply
        a choice. Property-specific template suppression uses <code>EmailProperty.SENDING_TO_ACCEPTED_RECIPIENTS</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-properties small"># A customizable default:
simplejavamail.defaults.sendtoacceptedrecipients=true

# Alternatively, keep the choice fixed within this factory:
simplejavamail.locked.defaults.sendtoacceptedrecipients=false</code></pre>
      <p>A <a href="configuration.html#section-locked-configuration">locked property</a> survives clearing, suppression and replacement templates.
        An equal explicit choice is allowed; a conflicting one fails. Exact Emails use the same setter as envelope-only metadata: they bypass ordinary
        templates but retain applicable locks, without changing their authoritative bytes.</p>
      <p>If the choice stays unset, existing provider settings remain in control. Angus's advanced <code>mail.smtp.sendpartial=true</code>
        (or <code>mail.smtps.sendpartial=true</code>) cannot be overridden by one Email choosing false. That clash fails before <code>MAIL FROM</code>.
        Remove the advanced property and use an Email default or the ordinary property above, so individual emails can still opt out.</p>
      <p>The bundled Angus adapter supports both choices. Another provider needs an adapter that explicitly supports them; otherwise the send fails
        before submission. <a href="features.html#section-custom-mailer">CustomMailer</a> receives the governed choice on Email and owns honoring it,
        but is incompatible with a factory lock on this setting because Simple Java Mail cannot check its internal recipient handling.</p>
      <p>See the <a href="spring.html#section-spring-recipient-rejection">Spring configuration example</a> and
        <a href="cli.html#section-cli-recipient-rejection">CLI options</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-recipient-details" id="section-recipient-details" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Inspect recipient details and server replies</h2>
  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>
        <code>getRecipientResults()</code> keeps one immutable result per envelope recipient occurrence, in envelope order, when the provider exposes
        that envelope. These are transport recipients, including Bcc or overridden envelope receivers, not just the visible To header. Repeated
        addresses can have different RCPT replies, so do not collapse the list into a map and discard occurrences.
      </p>
      <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable recipient result reference">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th><code>MailRecipientResult</code> method</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>getOriginalAddress()</code></td><td>The original transport address, including a display name when supplied.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>getEnvelopeAddress()</code></td><td>The optional mailbox form used by the transport.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>getDisposition()</code></td><td>Final submission knowledge: <code>ACCEPTED</code>, <code>VALID_UNSENT</code>, <code>INVALID</code> or <code>UNKNOWN</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>getRcptStatus()</code></td><td>What happened at RCPT TO: <code>ACCEPTED</code>, <code>TEMPORARILY_REJECTED</code>, <code>PERMANENTLY_REJECTED</code>, <code>NOT_ATTEMPTED</code> or <code>UNKNOWN</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>getRcptAttempted()</code></td><td><code>Optional&lt;Boolean&gt;</code>: true or false when known. Empty is unknown, not false.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>getRcptResponse()</code></td><td>The optional reply to this recipient's RCPT command, not a copy of the final DATA response.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        An address accepted at RCPT TO is not yet an accepted message. For example, the server could later reject DATA for policy reasons. In that
        case <code>getRcptStatus()</code> can be <code>ACCEPTED</code> while <code>getDisposition()</code> is <code>VALID_UNSENT</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        Both <code>receipt.getSmtpResponse()</code> and <code>recipient.getRcptResponse()</code> expose <code>SmtpServerResponse</code> when available.
        Its <code>getReturnCode()</code> is the primary code, such as <code>550</code>.
        <code>getEnhancedStatusCode()</code> optionally gives an <code>EnhancedStatusCode</code>, such as <code>5.1.1</code>,
        with <code>isTemporaryFailure()</code>, <code>isPermanentFailure()</code> and separate numeric components. It is the same type used by
        <a href="/delivery-reports.html">received delivery reports</a>. Missing, malformed, inconsistent or primary-code-mismatched enhanced statuses remain absent.
        Unfamiliar numeric combinations stay readable; the complete provider reply remains unchanged in <code>getResponse()</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>getResponse()</code> returns the raw server text, or <code>null</code> if absent.
        <code>isPositiveCompletionReply()</code> tests for a 2xx reply to that command. On a recipient result it says nothing about the later DATA step.
        Use the structured codes for decisions, not a search through the server's human-readable wording.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small" id="example-server-replies">for (MailRecipientResult recipient : receipt.getRecipientResults()) {
    recipient.getRcptResponse().ifPresent(reply -&gt;
        System.out.printf("RCPT: %d, enhanced=%s%n",
            reply.getReturnCode(),
            reply.getEnhancedStatusCode().map(Object::toString).orElse("unavailable")));
}

receipt.getSmtpResponse().ifPresent(reply -&gt;
    System.out.printf("Transaction: %d, enhanced=%s%n",
        reply.getReturnCode(),
        reply.getEnhancedStatusCode().map(Object::toString).orElse("unavailable")));</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-text small" id="output-server-replies">RCPT: 250, enhanced=2.1.5
RCPT: 450, enhanced=4.2.0
RCPT: 550, enhanced=5.1.1
Transaction: 250, enhanced=2.0.0</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-retry-decisions" id="section-retry-decisions" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Decide what can be retried</h2>
  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>
        <code>getRetryDisposition()</code> summarizes what the recorded SMTP facts allow you to conclude. It does not resend mail, pick a delay or
        set an attempt limit. “Safe” means those facts do not indicate duplicate submission for the candidates. It is not an exactly-once guarantee.
      </p>
      <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable retry guidance reference">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Retry disposition</th><th>Application decision</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>SAFE_TO_RETRY_ALL</code></td><td>The whole envelope is known unsent and the known facts permit a retry. Apply your backoff and attempt limits.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>SAFE_TO_RETRY_UNACCEPTED</code></td><td>Consider only <code>getRetryableRecipients()</code>, excluding anyone already accepted or permanently rejected.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>DUPLICATE_RISK</code></td><td>Acceptance is uncertain, or a candidate mailbox also appears as accepted. Reconcile the attempt before deciding to resend.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>CALLER_POLICY_REQUIRED</code></td><td>The provider facts do not support more specific advice. Use your application's knowledge of the send path.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>DO_NOT_RETRY</code></td><td>The send succeeded, the rejection requires a change, or no recipients qualify. Do not repeat this attempt unchanged.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        <code>getRetryableRecipients()</code> is populated only for the two safe-to-retry dispositions. It excludes permanent RCPT rejections and is
        empty for a permanent final DATA rejection or ambiguous acceptance. For our partial send, only Bob appears.
      </p>
      <p>
        This example just collects the candidates. Your retry job must use an envelope containing only those candidates, not resend the original
        email to Alice, Bob and Carol. Your application owns backoff, attempt limits, persistence and any message or address corrections.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small" id="example-retry-candidates">List&lt;String&gt; retryAddresses = receipt.getRetryableRecipients().stream()
    .map(recipient -&gt; recipient.getEnvelopeAddress()
        .orElse(recipient.getOriginalAddress()))
    .collect(Collectors.toList());

System.out.println("Retry candidates: " + retryAddresses);</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-text small" id="output-retry-candidates">Retry candidates: [bob@example.org]</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-unknown-acceptance" id="section-unknown-acceptance" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>When the final reply never arrives</h2>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        Now consider a separate attempt addressed only to Alice. Her RCPT command succeeds and the client sends the message data, but the connection
        drops before the final response arrives. The server may already have queued the message. A missing reply is not a rejection.
      </p>
      <p>
        The receipt is <code>UNKNOWN</code> with <code>DUPLICATE_RISK</code>, no final SMTP response and no automatic retry candidates. Even if the
        provider retained Alice's positive RCPT reply, it cannot prove final submission. Some failure paths also lose those earlier RCPT details;
        the receipt then leaves them unknown rather than guessing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Check server logs or provider tracking if available, or apply an explicit application policy for possible duplicates. Reusing a Message-ID
        does not by itself guarantee that an SMTP server will discard a second submission.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-text small">RCPT TO:&lt;alice@example.org&gt;  → 250 Recipient OK
DATA                         → 354 Send message data
[message data, then a dot]    → connection lost; no final reply</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-java small" id="example-unknown-acceptance">System.out.println("Submission: " + receipt.getStatus());
System.out.println("Retry: " + receipt.getRetryDisposition());
System.out.println("Final reply present: " + receipt.getSmtpResponse().isPresent());
System.out.println("Retry candidates: " + receipt.getRetryableRecipients().size());</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-text small" id="output-unknown-acceptance">Submission: UNKNOWN
Retry: DUPLICATE_RISK
Final reply present: false
Retry candidates: 0</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-control-results" id="section-control-results" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Interpret cancellation and timeout results</h2>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p><code>MailSendCancelledException</code> and <code>MailSendTimeoutException</code> say why an operation stopped.
        Their optional <code>getSubmissionReceipt()</code> says what SMTP facts were known. These are separate questions:
        a stop request does not recall mail or establish that it was unsent.</p>
      <p>Before execution or while claiming a connection there may be no receipt. With the managed Angus transport, a stop before the
        terminating DATA command is known unsent; a lost final reply after DATA termination or BDAT LAST is
        <code>UNKNOWN</code> with <code>DUPLICATE_RISK</code>. Recipient replies already captured remain available.
        If final acceptance was observed first, a late request can still leave a normal successful completion.</p>
      <p>The callback below sees the actual operation failure. <code>get()</code> and <code>join()</code> wrap it in
        ExecutionException and CompletionException, respectively. Cancelling the detached future itself affects only that view.
        For configuration, ownership, and the limitations of stopping I/O, see
        <a href="/sending-and-execution.html#section-send-deadlines">Deadlines and cancellation</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="side"><pre><code class="language-java">MailSend&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; send =
    mailer.async().sendMail(email);

send.getCompletion().whenComplete((receipt, failure) -&gt; {
    if (failure instanceof MailSendCancelledException) {
        System.out.println("Cancellation stopped remaining work");
        ((MailSendCancelledException) failure).getSubmissionReceipt()
            .ifPresent(MyDiagnostics::printReceipt);
    } else if (failure instanceof MailSendTimeoutException) {
        System.out.println("The send budget expired");
        ((MailSendTimeoutException) failure).getSubmissionReceipt()
            .ifPresent(MyDiagnostics::printReceipt);
    } else if (failure != null) {
        recordFailure(failure);
    } else {
        printReceipt(receipt);
    }
});

// For example, the campaign was withdrawn:
send.requestCancellation();</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-text">Cancellation stopped remaining work
Submission: UNKNOWN
Retry: DUPLICATE_RISK</code></pre>
      <p>This illustrative output means the final acceptance reply was not observed. Do not automatically retry the original email.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-other-send-paths" id="section-other-send-paths" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Use the same facts in other send paths</h2>
  <a href="#section-async-results" id="section-async-results" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Asynchronous sends</h3>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        <code>mailer.async().sendMail(email)</code> returns <code>MailSend&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt;</code>; its <code>getCompletion()</code>
        provides a detached CompletableFuture. Failed and partial submissions complete it exceptionally with <code>MailSubmissionException</code>. Chained stages can wrap that failure in
        <code>CompletionException</code>, so unwrap it before reading the receipt.
      </p>
      <p>
        This callback prints the same partial-send output as the synchronous example. <code>whenComplete</code> observes the result without
        recovering from the failure: <code>observedSend</code> still completes exceptionally for that partial send. Keep the Mailer open until
        outstanding sends complete, and use a thread-safe callback.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is no separate receipt-specific send method or configured execution-mode default. Choose <code>mailer.sync()</code> or <code>mailer.async()</code> per operation.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small" id="example-async-receipt">CompletableFuture&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; observedSend =
    mailer.async().sendMail(email).getCompletion().whenComplete((receipt, failure) -&gt; {
        if (failure == null) {
            printReceipt(receipt);
            return;
        }

        Throwable cause = failure;
        while (cause instanceof CompletionException &amp;&amp; cause.getCause() != null) {
            cause = cause.getCause();
        }
        if (cause instanceof MailSubmissionException) {
            printReceipt(((MailSubmissionException) cause).getSubmissionReceipt());
        } else {
            System.out.println("Failed without a submission receipt: "
                + cause.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }
    });</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-observer-results" id="section-observer-results" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Final mail-send results</h3>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        If you want one reporting point for ordinary sends, asynchronous sends and batches, configure <code>withMailSendResultObserver(...)</code> on
        the builder. Its <code>MailSendResultObserver.onMailSendCompleted(MailSendOutcome)</code> callback receives the whole attempt, including preparation
        and scheduling failures, rather than only SMTP facts. The optional <code>getSubmissionReceipt()</code> is the same receipt returned to the
        caller or attached to <code>MailSubmissionException</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>isSuccessful()</code> describes whether the configured send operation completed without throwing. In logging-only mode that can be
        true while the receipt is <code>UNKNOWN</code>, because no SMTP submission occurred. <code>isLoggingOnly()</code> reports the configured mode.
        <code>getFailure()</code> contains the attempt's failure when present.
      </p>
      <p>
        By default the observer runs before the synchronous call returns or throws, or before async completion. The executor overload instead
        attempts handoff before completion without waiting for the callback. For dispatch, ownership, timestamps, and the progress demos, see
        <a href="/sending-and-execution.html#section-mail-send-observer">the observer configuration guide</a>.
      </p>
      <p>When reusing a builder, <code>clearMailSendResultObserver()</code> removes its result callback and dispatch executor.
        <code>clearMailSendProgressObserver()</code> does the same for progress, independently. Neither changes Mailers already built from it.
        A no-op callback still enables observation; clearing the registration avoids that work when no other observation facility needs it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small" id="example-observer-receipt">mailerBuilder.withMailSendResultObserver(outcome -&gt; {
    String submission = outcome.getSubmissionReceipt()
        .map(receipt -&gt; receipt.getStatus().name())
        .orElse("none");

    System.out.printf("successful=%s, loggingOnly=%s, submission=%s%n",
        outcome.isSuccessful(), outcome.isLoggingOnly(), submission);
});</code></pre>
      <strong>Illustrative outcomes from separate attempts</strong>
<pre><code class="language-text small" id="output-observer-receipt">successful=false, loggingOnly=false, submission=PARTIALLY_ACCEPTED
successful=false, loggingOnly=false, submission=none
successful=true, loggingOnly=true, submission=UNKNOWN</code></pre>
      <p>The middle line could be a preparation or scheduling failure. It is not an unknown SMTP result: there is no receipt.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-live-progress" id="section-live-progress" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Watch a send while it runs</h3>
  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>A large attachment is taking a while. Is the email still waiting for a connection, uploading, or waiting for the server's answer?
        Register a progress observer for that live view, and keep a result observer for what finally happened. Either works on its own.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small">Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.org", 587)
    .withMailSendProgressObserver(progress -&gt; System.out.println(progress))
    .withMailSendResultObserver(outcome -&gt; {
        System.out.println("Result " + outcome.getObservationId()
            + ": successful=" + outcome.isSuccessful());
        outcome.getSubmissionReceipt().ifPresent(receipt -&gt;
            System.out.println("SMTP submission: " + receipt.getStatus()));
    })
    .buildMailer();

try (mailer) {
    mailer.async().sendMail(email).getCompletion().join();
}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>The snapshot has explicit getters: <code>getStage()</code>, <code>getElapsed()</code>, <code>getObservedAt()</code>,
        <code>getSequence()</code>, <code>getSubmissionAttempt()</code>, and optional <code>getBytesWritten()</code> and <code>getTotalBytes()</code>.
        Elapsed time uses a monotonic clock; the timestamp is for correlating external logs. The automatic string includes the observation ID,
        stage, attempt, elapsed time and available counts, but never Message-IDs, addresses, content, credentials or raw server replies.</p>
      <p>Here is abbreviated output for a send that waits for its rate allowance and connection, uploads, gets a temporary refusal,
        then succeeds on a configured retry. The ID is shortened here for readability; the real value is a UUID. Intermediate snapshots may be missing.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-text small">id=7cf5… seq=2  WAITING_FOR_RATE_LIMIT attempt=0 elapsed=PT0.02S
id=7cf5… seq=3  ACQUIRING_CONNECTION  attempt=0 elapsed=PT2S
id=7cf5… seq=6  UPLOADING             attempt=1 elapsed=PT4S bytes=524288/1048576
id=7cf5… seq=8  WAITING_FOR_RESPONSE  attempt=1 elapsed=PT4.5S bytes=1048576/1048576
id=7cf5… seq=10 WAITING_TO_RETRY      attempt=1 elapsed=PT4.8S
id=7cf5… seq=14 UPLOADING             attempt=2 elapsed=PT10S bytes=0/1048576
id=7cf5… seq=17 WAITING_FOR_RESPONSE  attempt=2 elapsed=PT11S bytes=1048576/1048576
Result 7cf5…: successful=true
SMTP submission: ACCEPTED
id=7cf5… seq=19 FINISHED              attempt=2 elapsed=PT11.2S</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="wide">
      <p><code>FINISHED</code> only means the logical send ended, not that it succeeded. The result, receipt and completion handle remain authoritative.
        A late progress callback may arrive after the result callback. A dashboard should remember finished IDs and ignore subsequent progress for them.
        The <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/blob/codex/10.0.0/modules/simple-java-mail/src/test/java/demo/MailSendObserverDemoApp.java">observer demo</a>
        shows both registrations and that terminal-state guard.</p>
      <h4>What the stages and upload figures mean</h4>
      <p><code>WAITING_FOR_EXECUTION</code> covers async admission and worker waiting; <code>PREPARING</code> covers email governance and validation.
        <code>WAITING_FOR_RATE_LIMIT</code> is intentional pacing, <code>ACQUIRING_CONNECTION</code> includes obtaining a usable connection, and
        <code>PREPARING_MIME</code> includes MIME conversion and protection. <code>SUBMITTING</code> is the provider invocation;
        <code>UPLOADING</code> and <code>WAITING_FOR_RESPONSE</code> add detail where supported. <code>WAITING_TO_RETRY</code> is a configured retry delay,
        and <code>CLEANING_UP</code> covers ordinary per-email release/close. Stages can repeat or be skipped. This is not a protocol-event audit trail.</p>
      <p>Attempt numbers start at one when the provider is invoked, not when a connection is tried. Connection failover leaves the number at zero;
        an actual retry increments it and resets upload counts. One email remains one observation throughout both.</p>
      <p>Managed Angus transports count serialized MIME bytes successfully handed to the DATA or BDAT content stream, before line-ending normalization,
        dot escaping, command framing and TLS overhead. The optional total comes from the existing size-preparation pass; it is not the receipt's canonical
        SMTP SIZE value. No extra content read is added. If content changes and the figures no longer agree, the total becomes unknown.</p>
      <p><strong>100% uploaded is not SMTP acceptance.</strong> Bytes may still be buffered, the final reply can reject the message, or the connection can
        disappear before acceptance is known. Final mailbox delivery remains separate. Caller-owned ordinary Angus transports, other adapters without
        upload support and <code>CustomMailer</code> report coarse submission progress with unknown byte counts. Logging-only sends report preparation,
        not invented SMTP activity. A lazy batch observes only emails it reaches; shared connection setup/teardown is not repeated for every email.</p>
      <p>For example, a slow greeting can leave a send in <code>WAITING_FOR_GREETING</code> before any submission attempt starts.
        Authentication has its own stage too. You do not need protocol debug logging to tell those waits apart.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-text small"># New connection; abbreviated illustrative snapshots, not a guaranteed event sequence
id=0ab2… seq=3  WAITING_FOR_GREETING       attempt=0 elapsed=PT0.1S
id=0ab2… seq=6  NEGOTIATING_CAPABILITIES   attempt=0 elapsed=PT2.4S
id=0ab2… seq=8  NEGOTIATING_TLS            attempt=0 elapsed=PT2.5S
id=0ab2… seq=12 AUTHENTICATING             attempt=0 elapsed=PT2.7S
id=0ab2… seq=16 SUBMITTING_SENDER          attempt=1 elapsed=PT5S
id=0ab2… seq=18 SUBMITTING_RECIPIENTS      attempt=1 elapsed=PT5.1S
id=0ab2… seq=20 WAITING_FOR_DATA_PERMISSION attempt=1 elapsed=PT5.2S
id=0ab2… seq=23 WAITING_FOR_RESPONSE       attempt=1 elapsed=PT5.4S bytes=2048/2048
Result 0ab2…: successful=true; SMTP submission: ACCEPTED

# Another email reuses a connection: no old greeting, TLS or authentication is replayed
id=39cd… seq=3 ACQUIRING_CONNECTION       attempt=0 elapsed=PT0.01S
id=39cd… seq=8 SUBMITTING_RECIPIENTS      attempt=1 elapsed=PT0.02S

# A provider without detailed hooks still reports the work SJM can observe
id=8ef1… seq=3 ACQUIRING_CONNECTION       attempt=0 elapsed=PT0.02S
id=8ef1… seq=6 SUBMITTING                 attempt=1 elapsed=PT0.4S
Result 8ef1…: successful=true</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="wide">
      <h4>Which connection and SMTP details are available?</h4>
      <p><code>NEGOTIATING_CAPABILITIES</code> covers EHLO or HELO, including EHLO after STARTTLS.
        <code>NEGOTIATING_TLS</code> covers an observable TLS upgrade, including its handshake and certificate checks.
        <code>SUBMITTING_SENDER</code> and <code>SUBMITTING_RECIPIENTS</code> describe the SMTP envelope exchange, not mailbox delivery.
        <code>WAITING_FOR_DATA_PERMISSION</code> means the server has not yet replied to DATA with permission to upload.</p>
      <p>BDAT does not have that DATA-permission exchange. Chunk replies are not reported as final acceptance waiting.
        DNS, TCP connection, proxy negotiation and implicit TLS stay within <code>ACQUIRING_CONNECTION</code> where the provider cannot
        identify them reliably. These stages are not per-phase duration measurements.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Sending path</th><th>What you can see</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>New managed Angus connection caused by this email</td><td>Supported greeting, capability, TLS and authentication stages, then envelope and upload progress</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Reused or background-created connection</td><td>Current envelope/upload work; no historical connection setup</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Shared batch/open-connection setup</td><td>Setup is outside individual emails; their own envelope/upload work can still be observed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Caller-owned ordinary Angus transport</td><td>Coarse progress; SJM does not replace your transport for diagnostics</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Another provider adapter</td><td>Any details it supports, with coarse fallback for the rest; setup and upload support are independent</td></tr>
          <tr><td>CustomMailer</td><td>Opaque submission callback, without invented SMTP detail</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>Missing detail never makes a provider unsuitable for sending. A fast exchange may also be coalesced before your callback sees it,
        so a missing stage does not prove that work was skipped. Handle additional enum values in dashboard switches; the stage list may grow.
        No commands, recipient addresses, credentials or raw replies are included.</p>
      <h4>Callback speed and shutdown</h4>
      <p>Progress runs on a lazily started, dedicated daemon dispatcher. The optional
        <code>withMailSendProgressObserver(observer, applicationExecutor)</code> overload hands work to your executor from that dispatcher, never from a send
        thread. Callbacks are serialized per Mailer. Sending, cancellation and completion never wait for a progress callback.
        Keep a supplied executor separate from mail-sending workers; if you deliberately share their executor, slow reporting can occupy those workers.</p>
      <p>Only the newest pending snapshot per ID is kept, up to 1,024 pending IDs plus one callback in flight; overflow discards the oldest pending snapshot.
        Upload-only updates are published at most four times per second per email. Stage changes and the final count bypass that throttle but can still
        be coalesced. These bounds keep a lagging dashboard from retaining an unbounded send history; they do not limit sending throughput.</p>
      <p>Callback runtime failures and rejected executor handoffs are logged and ignored, without retry or inline fallback. Shutdown waits for sending,
        not reporting: it discards pending progress and does not interrupt an executing callback or close your executor. Registering an observer again
        replaces only that observer and its dispatch choice. Both registrations are Java callbacks, not properties, Spring metadata or CLI options.</p>
      <h4>Three different identifiers</h4>
      <p>The observation UUID correlates progress and the final outcome for one invocation. Re-sending the same Email produces a new UUID, even if its
        Message-ID stays the same. <code>getInitialMessageId()</code> and <code>getEffectiveMessageId()</code> are separately available when known.
        The observation ID is never inserted into the email or SMTP exchange, is not ENVID, and is not a deduplication key or an OpenTelemetry TraceId.
        Older serialized outcomes can have no observation ID. Use it in logs or span attributes if useful, not as a per-send metric label.</p>
      <p>For completed phase durations, use <a href="#section-send-timings">actual-send diagnostics</a>. For a separate endpoint check rather than an email
        already in flight, use a <a href="/debugging.html#section-smtp-capabilities">connection probe</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-send-timings" id="section-send-timings" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Where did the time go?</h3>
  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>A send took a second and a half. Was it waiting for a worker or sending allowance, getting a connection, signing the email or submitting it?
        Your existing observer now receives <code>outcome.getDiagnostics()</code> automatically. These measurements describe that particular
        attempt, including failures before SMTP. Nothing is sent or read a second time to collect them.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small" id="example-send-timings">mailerBuilder.withMailSendResultObserver(outcome -&gt; {
    outcome.getDiagnostics().ifPresent(report -&gt; {
        log.info("{}", report);

        report.getRateLimitWait().getElapsed()
            .ifPresent(metrics::recordSendingLimitWait);
        report.getConnectionAcquisition().getElapsed()
            .ifPresent(metrics::recordConnectionAcquisition);
        report.getSubmission().getElapsed()
            .ifPresent(metrics::recordSubmission);
    });

    // SMTP acceptance remains a separate fact from whole-attempt success.
    outcome.getSubmissionReceipt().ifPresent(this::recordSubmissionResult);
    outcome.getFailure().ifPresent(this::recordFailure);
});</code></pre>
      <p><a href="/smtp-connection-failover.html#section-observation">Connection history examples</a> show a failed primary followed by an acquired
        failover connection. The same walkthrough includes an <a href="/smtp-connection-failover.html#section-retry">observer-based retry recipe</a>.
        Automatic failover stops at provider invocation; it never replays a partially accepted or uncertain submission.</p>
      <p><strong>Illustrative output:</strong> most of this attempt was spent getting a usable connection. The report uses Java's
        <code>Duration</code> notation: <code>PT0.9S</code> means 900 milliseconds.</p>
<pre><code class="language-text small" id="output-send-timings">Mail send timings:
  Total: PT1.4S
  Preparation: PT0.003S
  Scheduling: PT0.28S
  Sending-limit wait: not measured: No sending limit is configured for this destination.
  Connection acquisition: PT0.9S
  MIME preparation: PT0.034S
  Submission: PT0.165S
  Cleanup: PT0.002S</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="wide">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Getter</th><th>What it measures</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>getPreparation()</code></td><td>Selected-Mailer Email defaults/overrides and validation, accumulated across considered destinations. MIME conversion comes later.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>getScheduling()</code></td><td>Async admission and worker waiting, not just time sitting in the queue.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>getRateLimitWait()</code></td><td>Waiting for configured message or recipient allowance, separately from executor scheduling and connection acquisition.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>getConnectionAcquisition()</code></td><td>Getting a usable transport, including proxy startup, pool waiting and any connection setup or validation.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>getMimePreparation()</code></td><td>MIME conversion and protection using the selected Session.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>getSubmission()</code></td><td>The provider or custom-mailer call, including preflight, deferred reads, serialization, writes and replies. Not just network time.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>getCleanup()</code></td><td>Ordinary connection release, disposal or close, and associated proxy teardown. A healthy pool release need not close the socket.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>getConnectionHistory()</code></td><td>Bounded connection attempts and cached skips for this email; shared batch/open-connection setup is not repeated.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>If you configured <a href="sending-and-execution.html#section-sending-limits">sending limits</a>, a slow send may simply be waiting its turn.
        For example, with a spread-out 30-per-minute rule, the next worker might report:</p>
<pre><code class="language-text small">Sending-limit wait: PT1.8S
Connection acquisition: PT0.002S
Submission: PT0.11S</code></pre>
      <p>The limiter, not SMTP, caused most of that delay. No ordinary pooled connection is borrowed during the rate wait.
        A configured limit can also produce a measured zero; disabled limits are reported as not measured.</p>
      <p>Each getter returns a <code>MailSendMeasurement</code>. Its <code>getElapsed()</code> is present even when the measured duration is zero.
        Otherwise, <code>getUnavailableReason()</code> explains why it was not measured. For example, a synchronous send has no async scheduling step,
        and logging-only mode has no SMTP submission. Read the explanation as text, not as a status code to parse.</p>
      <p>The total uses a monotonic clock and stops before observer dispatch. It includes small gaps between the measured work, so the rows need not
        add up exactly. It is elapsed time, not CPU time or the remaining send-timeout budget. A slow inline observer can still delay your call or future;
        that application work is not included in its own report.</p>
      <p>Batch and open-connection emails use a connection opened outside their individual attempts. Their reports deliberately say so:</p>
<pre><code class="language-text small">Connection acquisition: not measured: The shared connection is opened and closed outside this email's attempt.
Cleanup: not measured: The shared connection is opened and closed outside this email's attempt.</code></pre>
      <p>The shared connection's eventual closing failure cannot be reported in an email outcome that has already been delivered.
        If opening a batch connection fails before any email is reached, there is no per-email outcome at all.
        Sending-limit waits still belong to each reached email and are measured, even though the batch or open-connection scope keeps its transport open.</p>
      <h4>Accepted by SMTP, then cleanup failed</h4>
      <p><code>isFailureObservedHere()</code> tells you whether the send failed during that step. For example, if submission fails and closing the connection
        also fails, only submission is marked. This identifies the failed step, not the underlying cause. No step is marked if the failure happened between
        measured steps. If SMTP accepts the email but releasing
        its connection fails, the observer keeps the captured receipt alongside the caller-facing failure. For example, an application combining
        the existing outcome fields with the cleanup measurement could print:</p>
<pre><code class="language-text small">successful=false, submission=ACCEPTED
Cleanup: PT0.002S (failure observed here)</code></pre>
      <p>Do not resend just because <code>isSuccessful()</code> is false. Check the receipt and retry guidance first. A secondary cleanup failure also
        cannot replace an earlier submission failure's location. No receipt is invented when the send never produced one.</p>
      <h4>Which server handled this attempt?</h4>
      <p><code>getSmtpHost()</code> and <code>getSmtpPort()</code> expose the logical endpoint reported by the selected transport when known.
        A cluster can select a different server from the invoking Mailer's configuration. Unknown fields stay empty; no port is guessed.
        These values are not a physical-peer address, a proxy route or proof of TLS. Custom mailers remain opaque.</p>
      <p>The default report omits endpoints, message identifiers, exception messages and content. Export endpoint fields only when appropriate for your
        application; avoid turning arbitrary hosts, Message-IDs or recipient addresses into unbounded metric labels.
        Older serialized or manually constructed outcomes may have no diagnostics.</p>
      <p>Use a <a href="/debugging.html#section-smtp-capabilities">connection probe</a> to inspect what a fresh connection advertises and negotiates.
        Use these timings to understand a real attempt, on the connection it actually used. The measurements work across providers without promising
        separate DNS, TLS, authentication or SMTP-command timings.</p>
      <p>The manually runnable
        <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/blob/codex/10.0.0/modules/simple-java-mail/src/test/java/demo/MailSendObserverDemoApp.java">MailSendObserverDemoApp</a>
        prints these measurements as concurrent emails finish. Enable <code>DemoAppBase.LOGGING_MODE</code> to try it without contacting SMTP.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-open-connection-results" id="section-open-connection-results" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Open connections and simple batches</h3>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        Inside <code>withOpenConnection(...)</code>, <code>MailSender.sendMail(email)</code> returns the same receipt synchronously while
        reusing the callback's connection. This is useful when a job needs to record each successful submission before advancing to the next email.
        The example prints the receipt and rethrows a failure so the callback stops.
      </p>
      <p>
        An asynchronous simple batch returns a <code>MailSend&lt;Void&gt;</code> handle; a synchronous batch waits and returns no value. Neither collects receipts.
        Use the configured observer for each email actually attempted.
        Batches stop at their first failure; untouched emails have no outcomes. Pooling changes how a connection is obtained, not how to read a
        receipt. <code>withOpenConnection</code> uses the built-in transport path and cannot be combined with a
        <a href="/features.html#section-custom-mailer">CustomMailer</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small" id="example-open-connection-receipt">mailer.withOpenConnection(sender -&gt; {
    try {
        printReceipt(sender.sendMail(email));
    } catch (MailSubmissionException failure) {
        printReceipt(failure.getSubmissionReceipt());
        throw failure;
    }
});</code></pre>
      <p>The same Alice/Bob/Carol attempt produces the earlier partial-send output, then the exception leaves the callback.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-provider-details" id="section-provider-details" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Account for missing provider details</h2>
  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>
        The API is provider-neutral, but the available evidence depends on the provider. Angus supplies individual RCPT replies when it can.
        Simple Java Mail collects its successful replies internally and restores the prior reporting setting after the send. You do not need to
        enable <code>mail.smtp.reportsuccess</code>; a fully accepted send returns normally even if that setting was already enabled.
      </p>
      <p>
        Other providers may expose fewer facts. Some connection failures prevent Angus from exposing earlier RCPT replies. An early abort at a
        repeated mailbox can also leave its exact envelope position unknown; individual RCPT facts then remain absent and retry advice requires
        caller policy. Logging-only mode and custom mailers expose no RCPT evidence and use <code>CALLER_POLICY_REQUIRED</code>.
        Older serialized receipts retain their known recipient buckets, but cannot recover original envelope order or RCPT details that were never stored.
      </p>
      <p>
        Receipts and recipient results are immutable snapshots of one attempt, including when a pooled transport is reused. They are not sanitized
        logging reports: addresses and raw server replies may contain personal information or control characters. The examples use fictional addresses
        and mostly print enums and codes. In application logs, decide which details to retain and escape untrusted text appropriately.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a problem before submission, start with <a href="/debugging.html#section-config-diagnostics">configuration provenance</a>,
        <a href="/debugging.html#section-async-queue">async queue diagnostics</a> or
        <a href="/debugging.html#section-debug-logging">Jakarta Mail debug logging</a>. For later delivery, use
        <a href="/features.html#section-delivery-status-notification">delivery status notifications</a>, bounces or provider-specific tracking;
        a submission receipt alone cannot tell you what happened in the recipient's mailbox.
      </p>
      <p>
        To identify the original destination in a later DSN, the managed Angus transport supplies
        <a href="/features.html#section-dsn-recipients">ORCPT and optional recipient-specific NOTIFY preferences</a> when supported.
        ORCPT can appear as <code>Original-Recipient</code> in that later report; it is not the immediate receipt's <code>getOriginalAddress()</code> field.
        To match a later DSN to your application record, retain <code>receipt.getEnvelopeId()</code>, the effective
        <a href="/features.html#section-dsn-envelope-id">envelope identifier (ENVID)</a>. The bundled Angus adapter generates one per send when DSN
        is supported, unless you set <code>fixingEnvelopeId(...)</code>. The value is <code>null</code> when no effective identifier is available.
        The observer exposes the same receipt through <code>outcome.getSubmissionReceipt()</code>, including for batches.
        A later report can return the value as <code>Original-Envelope-ID</code>. It is separate from Message-ID and does not prove acceptance or final delivery.
        Neither result waits for that report; your application still needs to receive and process it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-retry-history" id="section-retry-history" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>See attempts inside one logical send</h2>
  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>With an observer and a configured <a href="/mail-send-retries.html">retry policy</a>, the outcome's diagnostics expose
        <code>getSubmissionHistory()</code>, <code>getOmittedSubmissionSteps()</code> and <code>getRetryWait()</code>.
        Each step identifies a real provider invocation, its status, optional primary SMTP code, elapsed time and selected next delay.
        No intermediate receipt, recipient list or exception text is retained. Read the final outcome's receipt for recipient facts.</p>
      <p>You can log the report directly from your observer:</p>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <pre><code class="language-java small">outcome.getDiagnostics().ifPresent(report -&gt; log.info("{}", report));</code></pre>
      <pre><code class="language-text small">Mail send timings:
  ...
  Retry wait: PT30.006S
  ...
  Submission history:
    - Attempt 1: REJECTED (SMTP 451), submission=PT0.04S, next delay=PT30S; Temporary refusal; the entire email can be submitted again safely.
    - Attempt 2: ACCEPTED (SMTP 250), submission=PT0.06S</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>This abbreviated, illustrative output shows a temporary refusal followed by acceptance on the second attempt. The observer fires once,
        with the final successful outcome. The configured delay was 30 seconds; the retry-wait measurement also includes waiting for resumed
        execution, so it can be longer. Initial admission and scheduling are measured separately.</p>
      <p>The first 63 and latest steps are retained. An exhausted policy still throws the last genuine submission exception.
        Connection history records acquisition and skipped destinations; submission history records actual provider invocations.
        The <a href="/mail-send-retries.html#section-results">retry walkthrough</a> includes the full timing report and explains ownership.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
